# Error handling

Use the existing utilities in `@/lib/utils/error-handler` so logging,
observability, and user feedback have one owner.

## Choose one path

- Server Actions, API routes, database, and external services:
  `handleServerError`.
- User-facing client operations and hooks: `handleClientError`.
- Fire-and-forget work: `handleBackgroundError`.
- oRPC tuple results: `handleORPCError` from
  `@/lib/utils/orpc-error-handler`.
- React error boundaries: capture the error with Sentry directly.

Each utility logs to the console and captures exceptions as appropriate.
`handleClientError` also shows a toast unless `showToast: false` is set.
Do not call Sentry, `console.error`, or `toast.error` again for the same error.

## Rules

- Include a descriptive `logMessage` and safe debugging context such as IDs,
  operation names, or route parameters. Never include secrets or raw credentials.
- Give users a short, non-technical toast title. Keep expected validation and
  user-input errors out of Sentry when they are not application failures.
- Do not silently catch an error. Return a useful response, show feedback, or
  rethrow with `rethrow: true` when the caller must fail.
- Do not wrap a function that already owns handling for the same failure.
- Keep one handler per logical operation; avoid nested catch blocks and
  duplicate reporting.
